From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Thu, 26 Feb 2026 01:35:39 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: the per-model instruction sets, as SUPP_INSN_*

The machine description asked TARGET_M11_40_PLUS whether it could use
MUL, DIV, ASH, SOB or XOR, which conflates five separate questions into
one model comparison.  Each is its own SUPP_INSN_* now, and every
pattern that needs one asks for that one.

That is what the Soviet chips need in order to be describable at all --
they do not sit anywhere in the DEC line.  The UKNC's 1801BM2 does: it
is an 11/40 by every one of these five answers and has no FP11 either,
so it needs no model of its own and gets none.  (It differs in FIS,
which is a flag rather than a model, and in __pdp11_model, where it
now answers 40.)  Its relatives would need masks of their own: 1801BM1A
has SOB and XOR without MUL, 1801BM1G has MUL without DIV, and neither
shape exists in this back end's -m10/-m40/-m45.  Nothing here targets
them, so the masks are not carried for them.

* Instruction-set macros, and the machine description gated on them
* Shift expansion and assembly, and the prologue
* pdp11 test suite

Signed-off-by: Yuriy Shestakov <165710+yshestakov@users.noreply.github.com>

Includes what was a separate fix on top of this one, now part of it:

pdp11: a rotate ended its assembly with a statement separator

rotlhi3 emitted "ashc %2,%0\t; limit 2261", the tail meant as a comment.
For this assembler ";" does not start one -- comments are "#" and "/",
and ";" separates statements -- so gas read "limit 2261" as an
instruction and refused the file.  Any program whose compilation produced
a 16-bit rotate failed to assemble, including ones with no rotate written
anywhere in them: a modulo by a constant is enough, which is how this
turned up, in an example drawing a sine wave.

The comment is dropped rather than re-spelled: what "limit 2261" was
telling the reader has not survived to anyone still here.  The
instruction itself is right, ASHC on an odd register being the machine's
own way to rotate, and the "d" constraint is what keeps the register odd.
